<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">1 January in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">43 events that happened on 1 January, from 153 BCE to 2024.</p></header>
<div class="tih-pinned-card"><p class="tih-pinned-label">International day</p><p class="tih-pinned-title">New Year's Day</p><p class="tih-pinned-desc">Observed globally on the first day of the Gregorian calendar, New Year's Day marks the start of a new year and is one of the most widely celebrated public holidays in the world.</p></div>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">153 BCE <span class="tih-ago">2,178 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Roman consuls take office on 1 January for the first time</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s consuls began their year of office on 1 January rather than in March, a change made to speed armies to the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>. The date became the start of the civil year and, eventually, of the modern new year.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">45 BCE <span class="tih-ago">2,070 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Julius Caesar's Julian calendar comes into force</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The solar calendar devised for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s erratic lunisolar reckoning. With a leap day every fourth year, it governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for over sixteen centuries and underlies the Gregorian calendar used today.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-calendar">Julian calendar</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/42-bce">42 BCE</a> <span class="tih-ago">2,067 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Roman Senate deifies the murdered Julius Caesar</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Two years after his assassination, the Senate declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> a god of the Roman state as Divus Iulius. The honour let his adopted heir Octavian, the future Augustus, style himself 'son of a god'.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">404 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,622 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Monk Telemachus killed trying to halt gladiatorial games in Rome</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">According to Theodoret, the monk Telemachus leapt into the arena to separate fighting gladiators and was stoned to death by the crowd. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a> is said to have banned gladiatorial combat in response.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1001">1001 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,025 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Stephen I crowned as the first King of Hungary</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">By traditional reckoning Grand Prince Stephen was crowned with a crown sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sylvester-ii">Pope Sylvester II</a>, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> a Christian kingdom. Stephen's reign anchored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Christendom</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1502">1502 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">524 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Portuguese navigators enter Guanabara Bay and name it Rio de Janeiro</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Sailing along the Brazilian coast, a Portuguese expedition entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanabara-bay">Guanabara Bay</a> and, taking it for a river mouth, called it the 'River of January'. The city founded there in 1565 would become colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s capital.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1515">1515 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">511 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Francis I becomes King of France on the death of Louis XII</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Louis XII died without a son, and his twenty-year-old cousin and son-in-law Francis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a> succeeded him. Francis became a great patron of the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and a lifelong rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1600">1600 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">426 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Scotland begins its year on 1 January instead of 25 March</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">By decree of James VI, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> moved the start of its legal year from Lady Day to 1 January. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> did not follow until 1752, so for 150 years the two kingdoms numbered their years differently.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1651">1651 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">375 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Charles II crowned King of Scots at Scone</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Two years after his father's execution, Charles II was crowned at Scone by the Scottish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covenanters">Covenanters</a>, the last coronation held there. His bid to regain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> ended in defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> that September.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1700">1700 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">326 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Peter the Great's Russia adopts the Anno Domini year count</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>'s orders, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> abandoned counting years from the Creation and began numbering them from the birth of Christ, starting the year on 1 January. It was an early step in his drive to Westernise Russia.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1739">1739 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">287 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Jean-Baptiste Bouvet sights the world's most remote island</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The French navigator Bouvet de Lozier sighted the ice-capped island that bears his name in the South Atlantic. Lying about 1,600 km from Antarctica, Bouvet Island is the most isolated island on Earth.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1773">1773 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">253 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">John Newton's 'Amazing Grace' is first used at Olney</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The curate John Newton, a former slave-ship captain, introduced the hymn later known as 'Amazing Grace' at a New Year's Day service in Olney, Buckinghamshire. Newton later became an outspoken opponent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/union-flag" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/union-flag.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1776">1776 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">250 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Washington's army raises the Grand Union Flag at Prospect Hill</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">During the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, the Continental Army hoisted the Grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-flag">Union Flag</a> on Prospect Hill near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, Massachusetts. Combining thirteen stripes with the British Union, it served as the first flag of the united colonies.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1788">1788 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">238 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Daily Universal Register becomes The Times</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">John Walter's three-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> paper published its first issue under the title The Times. It went on to become <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s newspaper of record and one of the most influential journals in the world.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1801">1801 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">225 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Act of Union creates the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and Ireland took effect, abolishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Irish Parliament</a> and sending Irish members to Westminster. The arrangement lasted until the creation of the Irish Free State in 1922.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1801">1801 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">225 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Giuseppe Piazzi discovers Ceres from Palermo</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Searching the night sky from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> Observatory, Piazzi spotted a moving point of light: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceres">Ceres</a>, the first object found in the asteroid belt. Long classed as a planet, then an asteroid, it is now termed a dwarf planet.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1804">1804 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">222 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Haiti declares independence from France</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a> proclaimed the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonaives">Gonaïves</a> after more than a decade of revolution. It became the first state founded by formerly enslaved people and the second independent nation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1808">1808 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">218 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">United States bans the importation of enslaved Africans</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Act Prohibiting Importation of Slaves came into force, outlawing the transatlantic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> itself and the domestic slave trade continued until the Civil War.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1818">1818 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">208 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Mary Shelley's 'Frankenstein' is published anonymously</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">'Frankenstein; or, The Modern Prometheus' appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in three volumes without its author's name. Mary Shelley's tale of a scientist and his creation is often called the first science-fiction novel.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1834">1834 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">192 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Zollverein customs union takes effect in Germany</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, most German states abolished tariffs between them and formed a single customs area. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zollverein">Zollverein</a> bound their economies together and prepared the ground for political unification in 1871.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1863">1863 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">163 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Lincoln's Emancipation Proclamation comes into force</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a>'s proclamation declared enslaved people in the rebelling Confederate states free. It turned the Civil War into a war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and opened the Union army to Black soldiers.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1877">1877 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">149 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Queen Victoria proclaimed Empress of India at the Delhi Durbar</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At a vast imperial assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy</a> Lord Lytton proclaimed Victoria Empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The new title, devised by Disraeli, marked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>'s claim to succeed the Mughal emperors.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1890">1890 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">136 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Italy proclaims the colony of Eritrea</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> united its possessions on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coast, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a>, into the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>. It became the base for Italian ambitions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, checked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> in 1896.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1892">1892 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">134 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Ellis Island opens as America's main immigration station</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The federal immigration station on Ellis Island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Harbor processed its first arrivals, beginning with 17-year-old Annie Moore from Ireland. Some 12 million immigrants passed through it before 1954.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1901">1901 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">125 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Six British colonies federate as the Commonwealth of Australia</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a>, Victoria, Queensland, South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, Western Australia and Tasmania united under a federal constitution. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-barton">Edmund Barton</a> was sworn in as the first Prime Minister in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a>'s Centennial Park.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1912">1912 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">114 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Sun Yat-sen proclaims the Republic of China in Nanjing</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> was sworn in as provisional president of the new Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Six weeks later the last Qing emperor abdicated, ending more than two thousand years of imperial rule.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1914">1914 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">112 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">World's first scheduled airline flight crosses Tampa Bay</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The St Petersburg-Tampa Airboat Line flew its first paying passenger across Tampa Bay in a Benoist flying boat. The 23-minute hop launched scheduled commercial aviation, though the airline lasted only four months.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1942">1942 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">84 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Twenty-six Allied nations sign the Declaration by United Nations</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Meeting in Washington, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and others pledged to fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> together and make no separate peace. The term '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>' later named the post-war organisation.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1947">1947 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">79 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">American and British zones of Germany merge into the Bizone</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> combined the economic administration of their occupation zones in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The Bizone, joined by the French zone in 1949, became the nucleus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1956">1956 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">70 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Sudan becomes independent from Britain and Egypt</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>'s parliament ended the Anglo-Egyptian condominium and the country became a sovereign republic. It was among the first African states to gain independence after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1958">1958 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">68 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The European Economic Community comes into being</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-rome">Treaty of Rome</a> took effect, creating a common market among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and Luxembourg. The EEC was the forerunner of today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1959">1959 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">67 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Batista flees Cuba as Castro's revolution triumphs</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulgencio-batista">Fulgencio Batista</a> fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> in the early hours, handing victory to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>. The new revolutionary government later aligned with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, reshaping the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1970">1970 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">56 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Unix epoch: the zero point of computer time</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Midnight UTC on this date was later chosen as the reference point from which Unix and its descendants count time in seconds. The convention still underpins timekeeping on most of the world's computers and phones.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1983">1983 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">43 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">ARPANET switches to TCP/IP, the birth of the modern internet</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">On this 'flag day' the US defence research network ARPANET required all hosts to use the TCP/IP protocols. The common standard allowed separate networks to interconnect, forming the internet.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1984">1984 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">42 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Brunei becomes fully independent from Britain</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The oil-rich sultanate of Brunei ended nearly a century as a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> and became a sovereign state under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassanal-bolkiah">Hassanal Bolkiah</a>. It joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/association-of-southeast-asian-nations">ASEAN</a> within the week.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1989">1989 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">37 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Montreal Protocol to protect the ozone layer enters into force</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The treaty phasing out chlorofluorocarbons and other ozone-depleting chemicals came into effect. Ratified by every UN member, it is widely regarded as the most successful environmental agreement ever made.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1993">1993 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">33 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Czechoslovakia splits peacefully into two states</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-czechoslovakia">Velvet Divorce</a>', <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> dissolved by agreement into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. The separation was negotiated by politicians without a referendum and passed without violence.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1994">1994 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">32 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">NAFTA takes effect between the United States, Canada and Mexico</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-american-free-trade-agreement">North American Free Trade Agreement</a> came into force, phasing out most tariffs among the three countries and creating one of the world's largest free-trade areas. It was replaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-mexico-canada-agreement">USMCA</a> in 2020.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1995">1995 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">31 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The World Trade Organization is established</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The WTO began work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, succeeding the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade of 1947. Unlike GATT, it covered services and intellectual property and had a binding system for settling trade disputes.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1999">1999 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">27 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The euro is launched in eleven European countries</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The euro became the official currency of eleven EU states, used at first for electronic transactions and financial markets. National notes and coins remained in circulation for three more years.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/2002">2002 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">24 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Euro notes and coins enter circulation</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Euro banknotes and coins went into circulation in twelve countries, the largest currency changeover in history. Within two months the franc, mark, lira, peseta and other national currencies had ceased to be legal tender.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/2007">2007 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">19 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Bulgaria and Romania join the European Union</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The two Balkan states became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>'s 26th and 27th members. Their accession completed the EU's great eastward enlargement into the former communist bloc that began in 2004.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/2024">2024 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">2 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Powerful earthquake devastates Japan's Noto Peninsula</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A magnitude 7.5 earthquake struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishikawa-prefecture">Ishikawa Prefecture</a> on New Year's Day, triggering tsunami warnings, fires and landslides. Including later disaster-related deaths, more than 500 people lost their lives.</p></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 1 January <span class="n">11</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pope-alexander-vi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Pope Alexander VI</span><span class="tp">1431–1503</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/lorenzo-de-medici.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Lorenzo de' Medici</span><span class="tp">1449–1492</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sigismund-i-the-old.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Sigismund I the Old</span><span class="tp">1467–1548</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/huldrych-zwingli.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Huldrych Zwingli</span><span class="tp">1484–1531</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/christian-thomasius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/christian-thomasius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Christian Thomasius</span><span class="tp">1655–1728</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/anthony-wayne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/anthony-wayne.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Anthony Wayne</span><span class="tp">1745–1796</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/hong-xiuquan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hong-xiuquan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Hong Xiuquan</span><span class="tp">1814–1864</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/james-frazer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/james-frazer.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">James Frazer</span><span class="tp">1854–1941</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/thibaw-min"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/thibaw-min.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Thibaw Min</span><span class="tp">1859–1916</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/e-m-forster"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/e-m-forster.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">E. M. Forster</span><span class="tp">1879–1970</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/manuel-roxas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/manuel-roxas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Manuel Roxas</span><span class="tp">1892–1948</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 1 January <span class="n">8</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/william-of-volpiano"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">William of Volpiano</span><span class="tp">962–1031</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-navarre"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/charles-ii-of-navarre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Charles II of Navarre</span><span class="tp">1332–1387</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-xii-of-france.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Louis XII of France</span><span class="tp">1462–1515</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/christian-iii-of-denmark"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/christian-iii-of-denmark.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Christian III of Denmark</span><span class="tp">1503–1559</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/james-francis-edward-stuart"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/james-francis-edward-stuart.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">James Francis Edward Stuart</span><span class="tp">1688–1766</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-auguste-blanqui"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-auguste-blanqui.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Louis Auguste Blanqui</span><span class="tp">1805–1881</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/edwin-lutyens"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/edwin-lutyens.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Edwin Lutyens</span><span class="tp">1869–1944</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/vincent-auriol"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vincent-auriol.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Vincent Auriol</span><span class="tp">1884–1966</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
